title<-'A peripheral-blood single-strand DNA break atlas across cognitive disorders and Alzheimer pathology'
abstract<-sprintf("Single-strand DNA break (SSB) landscapes provide a genomic view of DNA damage, but their organization across cognitive disorders and Alzheimer disease (AD) pathology has not been resolved in clinically reviewed blood cohorts. We linked nucleotide-position SSB sequencing to manually reviewed records from 261 participants: 144 healthy controls, 89 with Core 1-positive AD pathology and 28 with Core 1-negative cognitive disorders. Within-gene annotation unions removed transcript-level duplicate counting, and feature counts were normalized to each complete deduplicated SSB library. The prespecified EXON analysis identified 360 disease-group associations after adjustment for age, sex, library yield and poly(A)-filter retention: 11 higher and 349 lower normalized signals. Fourteen EXON associations were concordant across both pathology groups relative to controls. Whole-gene sensitivity analysis identified 3,013 associations and supplied functional context for cellular organization and signaling. Transcript-oriented profiles resolved distinct structural patterns across 13 annotation classes and 150 bins. Independent regional models underwent fivefold nested cross-validation repeated five times; the EXON source model achieved an AUC of %.3f (95%% CI %.3f–%.3f). The direct AD–Other contrast and classifiers defined the separate pathology-specific question; no feature passed its prespecified correction threshold. This participant-linked atlas establishes regional and gene-level blood SSB organization across cognitive disease and separates disease-source associations from documented AD pathology.",ps$AUC,ps$CI_low,ps$CI_high)
intro<-c(
 "Cognitive disorders encompass clinical syndromes with different underlying pathologies. Blood-derived molecular profiles offer a route to characterize the systemic biological organization accompanying this spectrum. A useful atlas must distinguish the molecular features shared across cognitive disease from those associated with a defined pathology. The revised Alzheimer’s Association criteria provide a Core 1 biomarker axis for this distinction, alongside clinical syndrome and stage [3,4].",
 "Single-strand DNA breaks are a major class of DNA lesions and intermediates of repair. SSiNGLe enabled nucleotide-resolution mapping and revealed nonrandom distributions across genic and regulatory contexts [1]. Subsequent genome-wide damage profiling demonstrated information associated with mammalian age [2]. Together, these studies motivate examining blood SSB organization in cognitive disease, rather than presupposing a uniform increase in damage or transferring an age-prediction endpoint to an AD diagnosis endpoint.",
 "The central gap is the relationship between a participant-level SSB landscape, clinically reviewed cognitive disease and biomarker-supported AD pathology. Regional annotation totals and gene-specific library-normalized signals answer different questions. A regional yield metric cannot establish lesion probability, and a disease-associated gene does not automatically identify AD pathology. Explicit denominators, nonredundant gene annotations and separate source and pathology contrasts are therefore essential to a coherent clinical application.",
 "Here we establish a blood SSB atlas from the final author-reviewed, sequencing-linked cohort. EXON union counts constitute the prespecified primary feature space; whole-gene, intronic and TSS-window annotations provide sensitivity and complementary views. We quantify overall regional Ri, disease-group and direct pathology associations, transcript-oriented structural profiles, and internally validated regional scores. Participant-level clinical measures and stage labels connect these measurements to the clinical context, with each analysis retaining its observed sample size.")
gri<-rr[model=='technical'&region=='GENE'];er<-mm[task=='source'&region=='EXON'];ec<-cl[task=='source'&population=='Disease-source'&region=='EXON'&trait=='mmse_observed_min'];u<-rd(path('results/sensitivity/broad_direction_concordance.tsv'))
res<-c(
 '## Results',
 '### A participant-linked atlas separates regional yield from normalized genomic organization',
 "The final author-approved cohort comprised 261 independent participants: 144 HC, 89 AD and 28 Other. AD denotes the manually reviewed Core 1-positive pathology label; Other denotes Core 1-negative cognitive disorders, rather than all non-AD clinic attendees or a uniformly defined MCI group. The combined disease comparison therefore comprised 117 selected participants. The same 261-person universe was used for all regional reference calculations; no participants with unavailable Core 1 labels were added. Age, sex and technical covariates were complete in 259 participants (142 HC, 89 AD and 28 Other). Numeric contemporaneous MMSE was available in 100 disease-group participants, comprising 79 AD and 21 Other (Table 1; Supplementary Fig. S1).",
 sprintf("Nonredundant regional fractions mapped each library to GENE, TSS200, INTRON and EXON classes (Fig. 1A). These classes overlap and are not a composition constrained to sum to one. Regional Ri used the full unfiltered GENE total of 228,422,149 across the cohort and a common denominator B=%.9f. Individual regional distributions and descriptive Wilcoxon comparisons are shown in Fig. 1B–E. GENE Ri closely tracked complete-library position yield (Fig. 1F). After age, sex and technical adjustment, its ratio of Ri+1 was %.4f (95%% CI %.4f–%.4f; P=%s; n=%d). The four fully adjusted regional effects did not cross the prespecified four-test correction threshold (Fig. 1G). The regional layer thus provides a library-relative yield context for the feature-level map.",B,gri$ratio,gri$ratio_low,gri$ratio_high,pf(gri$P),gri$n),
 "The three-group age distribution is shown alongside regional measurements (Fig. 1H). All twelve adjusted regional contrasts are displayed together (Fig. 1G); none passed the prespecified correction family. The pooled age/sex versus full-technical regional effects are retained in Supplementary Fig. S25A. Fixed-feature mean log signals provided a second, depth-normalized summary, with per-decade age coefficients and intervals in Supplementary Fig. S25B. Age-by-source interactions, medians and detection fractions were reported separately (Supplementary Fig. S3). These analyses describe age structure without substituting age categories for continuous adjustment or interpreting a nonsignificant interaction as equivalence.",
 '### Exonic associations define a disease-related feature map shared across pathology strata',
 sprintf("The primary EXON analysis tested %d detected gene features and identified %d associations (%d higher and %d lower) in the combined disease comparison at BH-adjusted P<0.05. Complementary and sensitivity spaces yielded %s (Fig. 2A–D; Supplementary Data 1). Effects are differences on log2(1+SSB_LPKM), not unconditionally interpreted count fold changes. In the primary EXON space, CHKB-DT, RHBDF1 and TBC1D20 were the three highest-ranked associations, with coefficients %.3f, %.3f and %.3f, respectively. Their participant-level values and the three adjusted group contrasts are shown in Fig. 4A1–A3. Selection used this cohort and serves atlas representation, not independent validation.",primary$tested,primary$higher+primary$lower,primary$higher,primary$lower,sigtext,top$beta[1],top$beta[2],top$beta[3]),
 sprintf("The three-group EXON model identified %d AD–HC and %d Other–HC associations; %d were significant in both comparisons with concordant direction. Threshold-specific sets comprised %d AD–HC and %d Other–HC associations (Fig. 2E,G). The four-region counts for all three predefined contrasts are shown together (Fig. 2F), and the direct AD–Other contrast yielded no corrected feature discoveries. A significant HC contrast in one pathology group but not the other was treated as set membership, not proof of an AD–Other difference.",b[region=='EXON'&contrast=='Positive_vs_HC',higher+lower],b[region=='EXON'&contrast=='Negative_vs_HC',higher+lower],sum(shared$shared),sum(shared$positive_only),sum(shared$negative_only)),
 "Removing cross-gene shared intervals tested whether annotation ambiguity contributed to association direction (Fig. 2H). Complete blood-cell adjustment and the 10%/30% detection thresholds provided additional evidence families (Fig. 2I). The displayed percentages use the common tested primary associations as their denominators. Disease-source direction remained concordant in the tested sensitivity intersections, while corrected retention depended on the adjustment and feature set. Age-stratified results and their strict intersections are supplied independently for each region (Supplementary Figs. S19–S22). All 28 Other participants were paired to an AD participant under same-sex, maximum-three-year criteria, producing 28 pairs with a maximum realized age difference of two years. Matched, AV45-only and discordant record-excluded results address prespecified population sensitivities (Supplementary Fig. S7); they do not replace the full direct pathology contrast.",
 '### Transcript architecture and whole-gene sensitivity annotation organize the atlas',
 "The structural profile preserved 13 original transcript annotation classes and 150 bins in the 5′–3′ direction. Exact per-participant integer totals were divided by the new common GENE reference before group aggregation (Fig. 3A). Internal intron bins carried the largest annotation-summed profile Ri. Normalization by annotated-base exposure changed the localization emphasis, illustrating why a profile-Ri maximum cannot by itself identify an exposure-independent breakpoint hotspot (Supplementary Fig. S24). Participant-bootstrap intervals summarize pointwise uncertainty; full covariate-adjusted bin and region results accompany the profiles (Supplementary Fig. S8; Supplementary Data 1).",
 sprintf("The primary EXON association set mapped %d genes to %d STRING proteins against a background of %d actually tested, mapped proteins. None of the prespecified GO biological process, GO cellular component, Reactome or HPO categories passed the provider-adjusted threshold in that primary set (Supplementary Fig. S12). Separately labeled GENE sensitivity analysis mapped %d associated proteins against a background of %d and supplied functional context for the broader genomic map (Fig. 3C–F). Representative terms included cellular component organization, cell projection and Rho-GTPase signaling. HPO annotations describe database gene–phenotype links rather than clinical phenotypes demonstrated in this cohort. Length-stratified resampling assessed annotation-length dependence across the complete returned term family (Fig. 3B). The primary EXON and secondary whole-gene annotation conclusions are kept distinct.",map[set=='All',query_genes],map[set=='All',query_proteins],map[set=='All',background],gm[set=='All',query_proteins],gm[set=='All',background]),
 '### Regional scores resolve disease-source discrimination and its clinical scope',
 paste0("Four independent regional classifiers were trained on the same 259-person covariate-complete cohort using identical outer partitions. AUCs from mean held-out probabilities were ",auc_text('source')," (Fig. 4B). The primary EXON model therefore carried internally cross-validated disease-source information, with all preprocessing, feature selection and tuning confined to training folds. The independently trained AD–Other task used 117 participants and yielded ",auc_text('pathology')," (Fig. 4C). Class labels and score orientation were fixed in advance; no AUC was reversed or threshold changed to improve its appearance."),
 "Precision–recall curves use each task’s observed positive-class fraction as the baseline, including 89/117 for AD–Other (Fig. 4D1). Five repetition-specific AUCs describe validation stability on repeatedly partitioned participants, not independent biological replication (Fig. 4D2). Calibration and Brier scores describe probability behavior within the assembled cohort (Fig. 4H). Conditional participant-bootstrap intervals do not include model-refitting uncertainty or constitute external validation. Ri and a separate routine clinical model are provided as prespecified references (Supplementary Fig. S10).",
 sprintf("Source-model scores were related to concurrent observed MMSE in the 100 selected disease participants (Fig. 4E). For EXON, Spearman rho was %.3f (P=%s). Its adjusted MMSE coefficient per 0.1 score was %.3f points (95%% CI %.3f–%.3f; P=%s), with no member of the four-region adjusted family crossing its corrected threshold (Fig. 4F). Other prespecified cognitive, blood-count and biochemical associations retained their actual observed-pair n (Fig. 4G; Supplementary Figs. S16–S18). These analyses characterize clinical relationships rather than validate a cognition-severity biomarker.",ec$rho,pf(ec$P),er$beta*.1,er$CI_low*.1,er$CI_high*.1,pf(er$P)),
 "Within AD, the reviewed early/mild and moderate-or-greater labels formed an exploratory 18-versus-25 clinical-stage contrast. One MMSE value was unavailable, leaving 42 measured pairs for the stage-adjusted MMSE analysis; molecular stage analyses used 43 participants. Stage definitions and concurrent cognitive tests were treated as related clinical descriptions rather than independent confirmations (Supplementary Fig. S11; Supplementary Data 1).")
discussion<-c(
 '## Discussion',
 "This atlas links blood SSB organization to a clinically reviewed cognitive-disorder cohort on an explicit pathology axis. Its central contribution is a participant-level, annotation-resolved molecular resource: 360 primary exonic associations, complementary gene-body and intronic views, and structural profiles whose count, library and exposure denominators can be traced. Distinguishing these measurements makes the genomic distribution informative without requiring a single global damage direction.",
 "The primary EXON unions reduce repeated contribution from alternative-transcript annotations within a gene. Their association set is predominantly lower on the library-normalized model scale. This direction concerns the distribution of captured genomic SSB positions relative to each sample’s library; it does not imply reduced per-cell damage or faster repair. The gene-body sensitivity map identifies a wider genomic context and a functional layer that is explicitly secondary to the exonic analysis. The region-specific results demonstrate why genomic annotation choices must remain visible when discussing a blood damage landscape.",
 "A second contribution is the direct separation of disease-source and pathology questions. AD and Other both contribute associations relative to HC, and the shared exonic set identifies overlapping disease-associated organization. Direct AD–Other estimates and classifiers evaluate the additional distinction carried by the documented Core 1 labels. These results support a cognitive-disease atlas interpretation rather than an AD-specific diagnostic claim; the nonsignificant corrected direct contrasts do not prove molecular equivalence. The complete predeclared comparisons permit this interpretation without treating significance against a common reference as a test of group difference.",
 "The structural profiles extend the gene associations to transcript architecture. Annotation-summed intronic peaks and annotated-base-adjusted localization describe different aspects of the same mapped libraries. Exposure, transcript number and overlap must be considered alongside apparent peak height. Likewise, whole-gene ontology enrichment supplies testable cellular context rather than a demonstration of pathway activation, neuronal tissue origin or causality. Blood-cell sensitivity analyses further place feature associations in their systemic cellular context.",
 "Repeated nested validation establishes the internal disease-source information represented by regional profiles. Its design prevents held-out labels from entering molecular filtering or feature selection, and identical splits permit transparent model comparisons. Clinical score associations use concurrent observations and display the relevant n. The separate pathology task and the observed clinical associations define what these scores have demonstrated in this cohort: source discrimination, not a calibrated population AD risk or an established cognitive progression measure.",
 "The inference scope is that of a cross-sectional, single clinically assembled cohort. The analysis does not estimate causal damage pathways or within-person progression. Core 1 labels are author-reviewed clinical biomarker records; platform-specific thresholds and full imaging/CSF documentation require clinical confirmation. Unknown Core 1 records are outside the final author-approved 261-person cohort, so the pooled comparison is not the complete clinic-source population. Two HC lack age/sex covariates and were retained for descriptive summaries. Technical adjustment included library yield and poly(A) retention, not sequencing-run indicators; residual recruitment, processing and leukocyte-composition differences may contribute to source associations. Unequal pathology groups and conditional fixed-prediction bootstrap intervals constrain diagnostic extrapolation. Prospective independent cohorts and orthogonal locus-level assays are the appropriate next tests.",
 '## Conclusion',
 "The study provides a reproducible blood SSB atlas connecting nonredundant exonic features, complementary genomic annotations and transcript structure to reviewed cognitive disease and documented AD pathology. Explicit participant linkage, denominators and direct pathology contrasts make it a resource for testing how systemic DNA-break organization relates to clinical and biological dimensions of cognitive disorders.")
methods<-c(
 '## Methods',
 '### Study design, clinical records and analysis groups',
 "The study was an observational reanalysis of completed peripheral-blood SSB sequencing linked to manually reviewed clinical records. Participants, not genes, transcripts or bins, were the independent observational units. The final supplied three-group list was the sole inclusion authority after exact sample-ID linkage to the existing sequencing QC manifest. It contained 144 HC, 89 AD and 28 Other. Wider eligibility flags from the 171-record disease source file were retained for audit but did not add participants to this analysis. Manual exclusion records, failed QC linkage and unavailable Core 1 labels remained outside the final universe.",
 "Clinical records, concurrent cognitive assessments and available MRI, amyloid PET and CSF observations were retained with reference to clinical AD guidance and the 2024 revised criteria [3,4]. HC were participants without a documented definite cognitive-disorder diagnosis or marked abnormality on cognitive screening. Final molecular comparisons used the author-reviewed Core 1 axis: AD was Core 1-positive AD pathology, and Other was Core 1-negative cognitive disorders. These labels did not automatically redefine MCI as dementia or combine Other into a uniform MCI syndrome. Clinical diagnosis, reviewed phenotype and clinical stage remained separate variables. The final AD label consequently includes author-approved AD-spectrum cognitive presentations; it is not synonymous with probable AD dementia. HC were not assumed to be biomarker-negative.",
 "Sample collection and cognitive assessment were contemporaneous according to the study team. Numeric scores from the current reviewed disease records were used; missing entries, slash placeholders and non-cooperation without a measured number were missing, not zero. Corrected HC sample-to-sequencing linkage and the previously verified HC observations were retained. No disease score was filled from an older assessment. Observed Table 1 values show missingness. Analyses used complete required covariates or observed clinical pairs; prediction-only imputation was fitted within the relevant training fold. Original identifiable clinical files were retained in a restricted archive and excluded from publication and public-code packages.",
 "discordant record remained Core 1-positive under the final author label and was excluded, not relabeled negative, in a discordance sensitivity analysis. The AV45-only sensitivity used explicitly recorded positive and negative PET observations. Core 1 status was not substituted by MRI, APOE, MMSE or nonspecific serum measurements. Clinical-stage labels were copied from the reviewed exploratory-C fields: early/mild (18) and moderate-or-greater (25) formed the prespecified exploratory contrast; early cognitive disorder/MCI and unspecified stages were not forced onto an ordinal sequence. Full assay thresholds, ethics identifiers, recruitment dates and clinical adjudication personnel are study-specific author-confirmation items, not inferred computational facts.",
 '### Sequencing inputs, complete library denominators and coordinates',
 "The computational starting point was the completed upstream strand-aware one-base deduplicated BED, not a new FASTQ alignment. The reference genome and annotation were fixed to GRCh38.p14 and GENCODE v48. The upstream script’s documented defaults included BWA alignment, MAPQ 20, SSB adapter/flag selection and an endogenous-poly(A) check with a 40-base window and 0.4 threshold. Sample-specific upstream logs and commands should be consulted to confirm those settings for the deposited sequencing run. The rerun used the resulting valid BED positions unchanged except for verifying uniqueness.",
 "A unique SSB position was defined by chromosome, start coordinate and strand. Opposite strands at the same coordinate remained distinct; all valid represented contigs and non-genic positions were included in the denominator D_j. Duplicate keys were removed if present, and the audit found zero duplicate rows requiring removal. Every input was required to have end−start=1, nonnegative starts, an allowed strand and a positive library total. Complete chromosome totals independently reconstructed D_j. Unobserved values from successful processing were zero; failures or unavailable measurements were NA and blocked formal molecular inference.",
 '### Nonredundant regional count matrices',
 "GENE comprised the within-gene genomic interval union; EXON the union of exons across all transcripts of that gene; INTRON the union of transcript introns; and TSS200 the union of existing TSS-centered 401-base windows (approximately 200 bases on either side). Gene IDs, including the fixed annotation version, were matrix keys; symbols were used for display. Source GENE/EXON/INTRON closed intervals were converted by subtracting one from the start to form zero-based half-open BED coordinates, preserving one-base intervals. TSS coordinates retained the original BED window definition. Reconstructing unions from the source annotation matched the cached intervals in all four spaces.",
 "Intervals were merged within gene ID and chromosome before position counting. A unique position could count at most once per gene within one region. Overlapping different genes could each receive it. Disjoint coverage segments separately recorded cross-gene ambiguity; an unshared sensitivity matrix removed positions overlapping multiple genes. Exonic and intronic unions can overlap because different transcripts assign different structures to the same genomic bases. Both gene-annotation totals and global once-per-region position totals were exported. Gene-level counts were required not to exceed the complete library total, but summing across overlapping genes was not required to equal the library count.",
 '### SSB_LPKM and Ri',
 "SSB_LPKM followed the author-confirmed method formula adopted from the foundational damage-profiling study [2]. For region r, R(r)_ij is the nonredundant position count for gene i in sample j, and D_j is the complete valid deduplicated library count:",
 'EQUATION: SSB_LPKM(r)_ij = R(r)_ij × 10^10 / D_j',
 'EQUATION: Y(r)_ij = log2[1 + SSB_LPKM(r)_ij]',
 "This formula is a library-depth normalization without an explicit length term. No gene/exon-length division or TPM transformation was added, and the value was not termed breaks per kilobase. Region union lengths were retained for detection/bias checks and enrichment sensitivity. Raw count, raw SSB_LPKM and log-transformed matrices were saved separately. Heat-map Z-scores were a display transformation, not analysis inputs.",
 "Overall regional Ri was computed before detection filtering using all genes and the same full-cohort GENE reference for all comparisons:",
 'EQUATION: T(r)_j = Σ_i R(r)_ij;   B = [Σ_(j=1)^261 T(GENE)_j] / 261',
 sprintf('EQUATION: Ri(r)_j = T(r)_j / B;   B = %.12f',B),
 "Only the full-cohort GENE mean Ri is constrained to one. Other regions, clinical subgroups, matched participants and age strata retain this fixed B; they are not separately centered. Ri is the specified cohort-relative annotation count burden, not an absolute lesion probability or a forced 0–1 index. The reference totals can include cross-gene multiplicity. This project-specific Ri definition is distinguished from other enrichment or normalized-score metrics used in the original SSiNGLe report [1].",
 '### Differential association models and multiplicity',
 "EXON was the primary gene-feature space, GENE the whole-gene sensitivity space, and INTRON/TSS200 complementary spaces. Within each analysis population, a feature was eligible when its raw count was nonzero in at least 20% of valid independent participants. Three-group comparisons shared the same within-region tested set. Sensitivities used 10% and 30% detection. The primary source comparison used complete covariates in 259 participants. limma trend and robust empirical-Bayes moderation supplied feature-specific inference [5]:",
 'EQUATION: Y_ij = α_i + β_i Case_j + γ_i Age_j + δ_i Sex_j + η_i log10(D_j) + κ_i Retention_j + ε_ij',
 'EQUATION: Y_ij = α_i + β_AD,i I(AD)_j + β_Other,i I(Other)_j + γ_i Age_j + δ_i Sex_j + η_i log10(D_j) + κ_i Retention_j + ε_ij',
 "Case was one for AD plus Other and zero for HC. The three-group model used HC as reference and predefined AD–HC, Other–HC and AD–Other contrasts. Sex coding, design columns and actual sample IDs accompanied each fit. A reduced age/sex model described technical-adjustment dependence; it did not replace the primary technical model. Blood-cell sensitivity added available leukocyte measurements in complete cases. Age strata were 50–59, 60–69 and ≥70, retaining continuous age within each stratum.",
 'EQUATION: β_AD−Other,i = β_AD,i − β_Other,i;   CI_i = β_i ± t_(0.975,df_i) SE_i',
 "BH correction was applied separately within each prespecified region and contrast [6]. All tested features, effects, intervals, nominal P, adjusted P and sample n were retained. Significant features had corrected P<0.05. Colors use that threshold, while graphical numerical labels display nominal P as requested. The nominal P=0.05 reference in volcano plots is not the corrected selection threshold. Shared sets require both HC contrasts to pass correction and have the same effect sign; threshold-specific sets do not establish a direct difference. Empty corrected sets remain empty.",
 "Descriptive regional boxes show all participants, the median, IQR and whiskers to observations within 1.5 IQR. Group tests were two-sided unpaired Wilcoxon tests, with a separate correction family for four pooled comparisons and twelve three-group comparisons. Formal regional models used log2(Ri+1) with the same adjustment sets. Ratios shown in the forest plot are 2^β on the Ri+1 scale, not an untransformed Ri ratio. Complete-group regional comparison n and descriptive n were distinguished.",
 '### Age and matching sensitivity analyses',
 "The fixed detected feature set in each region generated sample mean log signals, medians and detection fractions. Mean log signal was the primary age summary because many medians can be zero. Age effects were per ten years and adjusted for source, sex, log10 D and retention. Age-by-source interactions were fitted separately. Four regional age effects formed one BH family; four interactions formed another:",
 'EQUATION: MeanLog_j = a + b(Age_j/10) + c Case_j + d Sex_j + e log10(D_j) + f Retention_j + ε_j',
 "Matching used Core 1-negative participants as anchors, same sex, absolute age difference at most three years and no replacement. A maximum-cardinality assignment with minimum summed age difference yielded 28 pairs. Paired regression included pair effects and technical covariates. Model folds for matched classifiers kept each pair together in training or held-out data. AV45-only, discordant record-excluded, detection and unshared-annotation analyses remained labeled sensitivities and did not change the primary groups.",
 '### Transcript-oriented profiling',
 "Profiling_File2’s original 13 classes and 150 bins were retained: TSS-upstream distance segments, first exon/intron, concatenated internal exons/introns, last exon, and TES-downstream distance segments spanning up to 5 kb. Strand orientation followed transcripts from 5′ to 3′. Internal segments were standardized transcript structures, not one physical genomic segment shared by every isoform. Exact integer per-sample bin totals were reaggregated; the rounded transcript mean-count matrix was not used to recover totals:",
 'EQUATION: C_jrb = Σ_t C_jtrb;   ProfileRi_jrb = C_jrb / B',
 'EQUATION: ProfileRi_jr = Σ_b ProfileRi_jrb;   ExposureDensity_jrb = 10^6 C_jrb / E_rb',
 "E_rb is the summed annotated base exposure for the original region/bin structure, including transcript multiplicity. This density is distinct from Ri and SSB_LPKM and does not include a separate library-depth divisor. A legacy up200_TSS class is the upstream 200 bp segment, not the 401-base TSS200 count-matrix window. The full profile may include flanking intervals, repeated transcript exposure and annotation overlap, so its total need not equal GENE Ri.",
 "Group means were computed after participant-level curves. Two thousand participant resamples within each group provided pointwise percentile 95% intervals. Transcripts and bins were not independent patients. Adjusted log2(ProfileRi+1) fits used the same covariates. The pooled task corrected 150 bins together and 13 regional sums separately; the pathology task corrected 450 bin contrasts together and 39 regional contrasts separately. Full integer inputs, exposure, group curves, effect intervals and observed model n were exported.",
 '### Functional annotation and length robustness',
 "STRING version 12.5 mapping and functional-enrichment endpoints were used for human genes [8]. The background was the set of actually tested features that mapped successfully, not all annotated human genes. Primary EXON and secondary GENE sensitivity queries were kept separate, each split into the complete, higher and lower significant sets. GO biological process, GO cellular component, Reactome and HPO categories were prespecified; HPO terms required HP identifiers. At most five provider-corrected significant terms per category were displayed. Insufficient mapping and categories without corrected terms were explicitly reported.",
 "Length robustness used 2,000 gene-set draws stratified by annotation union length against the corresponding mapped tested universe. Full reference annotations provided each term’s background membership, and observed overlaps were checked against the enrichment response. Empirical P and BH-adjusted empirical P were computed across the complete returned term family, rather than only the displayed five terms:",
 'EQUATION: P_empirical = [1 + Σ_(k=1)^2000 I(Overlap_k ≥ Overlap_observed)] / 2001',
 "Functional annotations describe gene-set context in blood-derived DNA. They were not interpreted as tissue-specific expression, pathway activation or patient-level phenotype validation. Direct-pathology enrichment required at least 20 mapped corrected features; that gate was not met.",
 '### Independently trained regional models',
 "Source models compared HC with AD plus Other in 259 complete-covariate participants. Pathology models compared AD with Other in 117 participants. Each annotation space was an independent model; there was no molecular–clinical joint model and no age-prediction model. A fivefold outer validation was repeated five times, with fourfold inner tuning. The same task-specific partitions were used across regions. Class labels and probability orientation were fixed to disease/AD as positive.",
 "Every inner training split independently fitted feature detection, finite-value checks, median imputation if necessary, nuisance regression, standardization and feature ranking. The same operations were refitted on the full outer training split before application to the outer test fold. Molecular nuisance regressions used age, sex, log10 D and retention; only their training coefficients were applied to held-out observations. Molecular detection required nonzero signal in at least 20% of that training split. Constant features were removed. Features were ranked using the absolute standardized two-class training mean contrast. Ridge-logistic models used glmnet with alpha=0 [7]:",
 'EQUATION: Z_train,ij = (X_train,ij − μ_train,i) / s_train,i;   Z_test,ij = (X_test,ij − μ_train,i) / s_train,i',
 'EQUATION: Rank_i = |mean(Z_i|y=1) − mean(Z_i|y=0)| / sqrt[s²_(i,1)/n_1 + s²_(i,0)/n_0]',
 "The inner grid combined the top 10, 25 or 50 features with lambda values 10, 1 and 0.1. The highest inner held-out AUC selected settings, with ties favoring fewer features and stronger regularization. No full-cohort differential list was used to prescreen prediction features. Separate reference models used regional Ri/count totals or age, sex and routine blood counts. The Ri reference in prediction was recomputed from each training fold’s GENE total to avoid using the held-out cohort mean. Clinical prediction excluded MMSE and diagnosis-derived variables; its missing values used training medians.",
 'EQUATION: p_mean,j = (1/5) Σ_(k=1)^5 p_OOF,jk;   Brier = mean_j[(y_j − p_mean,j)²]',
 "Primary AUC used each participant’s mean of five outer-held-out probabilities. Repetition-specific AUCs were also retained and not called independent replicates. Two thousand class-stratified participant bootstraps of the fixed mean held-out probabilities supplied conditional percentile AUC intervals; the procedure did not refit models and was not external validation. Average precision used tied-score grouped thresholds; its baseline was the observed positive fraction. Calibration used probability quantile bins and exported actual bin n. Fold manifests explicitly checked inner/outer participant disjointness and matched-pair blocks. Reporting separates endpoint, validation design and clinical-use claims in accordance with TRIPOD+AI [9].",
 '### Clinical associations, software and reproducibility',
 "Clinical analyses used observed concurrent pairs and required at least 20 complete observations and at least three distinct values in both variables. Spearman correlations were two-sided. Adjusted associations used a standardized clinical predictor with age, sex, log10 D and retention; pooled disease analyses also adjusted pathology group, and whole-cohort analyses adjusted source. Covariates without within-population variation were dropped and recorded. Separate populations were all, pooled disease, AD and Other. Actual participant lists and n accompanied every coefficient. Whole clinical association families were BH-corrected within task/population; a separate four-region MMSE-outcome family tested MMSE against source score, and another tested pathology score. Score intervals are conditional on the existing cross-fitted predictions.",
 "Direct regional mean/Ri and representative EXON-feature associations were exported separately from classifier-score associations. Clinical-stage comparisons adjusted the same technical and demographic covariates. Stage–cognitive-score relationships were descriptive clinical characterization, not independent evidence for the stage definition. The primary Table 1 used observed medians [IQR] or n/N (%) with missing counts and units; continuous group tests were Wilcoxon and sex tests Fisher exact.",
 "All downstream analysis, figure creation, Excel output and document assembly used R 4.5.3; package versions, input checksums, scripts, panel source data and random seeds are supplied. The master seed was 20261013. Position-count jobs used at most 12 workers, model jobs at most eight, and each worker used one BLAS/data.table thread; the workflow stayed below the requested 30-thread ceiling. Single panels and review composites use Arial 12 pt; submission composites use 8.5 pt at 183 mm width and at most 250 mm height. Main lines are 1.5 pt. PDF/SVG are editable vector outputs; separate TIFFs are 600 dpi. Raster resolution is not assigned to vector PDFs.")
